    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In a presidential democracy the people elect a president who heads the government for a fixed term and, unlike a prime minister, cannot be dismissed by the legislature for losing its support.</li><li>Devised by the framers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a> in 1787, it rests on the separation of executive, legislative and judicial powers, each able to check the others.</li><li>It spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> in the 19th century; Juan Linz argued that its rigid terms and winner-takes-all elections endanger democracy, a view his critics dispute.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Under the original Constitution the runner-up in the electoral college became vice-president, so in 1796 John Adams found his chief rival, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, serving as his deputy; the Twelfth Amendment of 1804 ended the arrangement.</p></aside>
    </section>

