    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states.jpg" alt="Constitution of the United States" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constitution_of_the_United_States_-_DPLA_-_9ca804144bd5965e992ae3528bc3c6a3_(page_1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Constitutional Convention, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In a presidential democracy the head of government is elected separately from the legislature and does not depend on its confidence. The president, usually head of state as well, is chosen by the voters, directly or, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, through an electoral college, for a fixed term, and directs the executive branch, appointing ministers or secretaries who answer to the president rather than to the legislature. The legislature, often of two chambers, is elected separately; it cannot remove the president for political disagreement, only through impeachment for serious wrongdoing or, in some constitutions, for incapacity, and the president cannot dissolve it. The political scientists Matthew Shugart and John Carey define the form by four features: a popularly elected chief executive, fixed terms for president and assembly that do not depend on each other’s confidence, a president who names and directs the cabinet, and a president with some constitutional power over legislation.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a>, the model for most later presidential systems, divides the legislative, executive and judicial powers among three branches and gives each the means to check the others. The president can veto bills, but Congress can override a veto by two-thirds of each house; the president commands the armed forces and conducts foreign policy, but Congress declares war and controls taxation and spending, and treaties need the consent of two-thirds of the Senate; the president nominates judges and senior officials, but the Senate must confirm them. The House of Representatives can impeach the president for “Treason, Bribery, or other high Crimes and Misdemeanors”, and the Senate tries the case, needing two-thirds to convict. No member of Congress may hold executive office at the same time, and since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marbury-v-madison">Marbury v. Madison</a> in 1803 the courts have claimed the power to strike down laws that violate the Constitution.</p><p>Presidential democracy differs from parliamentary democracy, in which the government is drawn from and can be dismissed by the legislature, and from semi-presidential systems such as the French Fifth Republic, which combine an elected president with a prime minister answerable to parliament. It also differs from the presidencies of dictatorships and one-party states, whose leaders carry the title without free elections or effective checks. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American constitutions give presidents more power than the American model, such as the right to issue decrees with the force of law or to veto parts of a bill, and many limit or forbid re-election.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton.jpg" alt="Alexander Hamilton" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Hamilton_(NPG).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Trumbull / Giuseppe Ceracchi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The presidential system was invented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, but from older materials. In the English colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> elected assemblies sat beside governors, appointed by the Crown or the proprietors in most colonies but chosen by the colonists themselves in a few, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and Rhode Island, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the Instrument of Government of 1653 had set a Lord Protector beside Parliament. Political theory supplied the principle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>’s Two Treatises of Government of 1690 distinguished legislative from executive power, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> argued in The Spirit of the Laws of 1748 that liberty was lost if the same person or body both made the laws and executed them. After independence in 1776 most American states kept their governors weak and chosen by the legislature, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1777 had its governor elected by the voters, and Massachusetts in 1780 gave an elected governor a veto.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a>, in force from 1781, had no executive at all, and the Congress could neither tax nor enforce its decisions. At the Constitutional Convention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1787 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> Plan proposed an executive chosen by the legislature. James Wilson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> argued for a single executive chosen by the people, holding that one person would be more energetic and more clearly accountable than a council, while Edmund Randolph warned that a single executive would be “the foetus of monarchy”. The delegates settled on one president, chosen for four years by electors appointed in each state as its legislature directed, eligible for re-election, armed with a veto that Congress could override, and removable by impeachment. They took the title from the presiding officers of colonial councils and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a>.</p><p>In The Federalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a> defended the new office: “Energy in the Executive is a leading character in the definition of good government.” <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> explained the separation of powers by human nature: since men were not angels, government had to be made to control itself, and “Ambition must be made to counteract ambition.” The Constitution took effect on 4 March 1789, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> took the oath as the first president in New York on 30 April.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-washington"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-washington.jpg" alt="George Washington" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilbert_Stuart_Williamstown_Portrait_of_George_Washington.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gilbert Stuart, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the office grew with the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>’s refusal of a third term in 1796 set a custom that held until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> won four elections from 1932 to 1944; the Twenty-second Amendment of 1951 then limited presidents to two terms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>, elected in 1828, vetoed more bills than all his predecessors together and claimed to speak for the whole people; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> stretched the war powers to fight the American Civil War; and in 1868 the House impeached Andrew Johnson, whom the Senate acquitted by a single vote. In the 20th century the presidency became the centre of the federal government, and in 1974 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> resigned rather than face impeachment over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watergate-scandal">Watergate scandal</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> took up the model when it won independence in the 1810s and 1820s, and presidential republics became the rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>’s federal constitution of 1824, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>’s of 1853, closely modelled on that of the United States, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>’s after the fall of its monarchy in 1889, when Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deodoro-da-fonseca">Deodoro da Fonseca</a> became its first president. Practice often departed from the text. Many presidents ruled as caudillos, extended their own terms or were removed by revolts and coups; Mexico’s revolution against the three-decade rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a> produced the constitution of 1917, which forbids any president a second term. Chile saw nearly sixty years of orderly presidential successions under its constitution of 1833, until a conflict between President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-manuel-balmaceda">José Manuel Balmaceda</a> and Congress ended in civil war in 1891, and Costa Rica, which abolished its army in 1948–1949, has elected its presidents without interruption since then.</p><p>Beyond the Americas, Liberia adopted it in 1847, and in the 20th century it spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and many newly independent states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, where it often became the frame of one-party rule. A wave of military coups in the 1960s and 1970s overthrew elected governments in Brazil in 1964, in Chile in 1973, after four decades of constitutional rule, and in Argentina in 1976. From the 1980s democracy returned across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> adopted direct presidential elections in 1987 after mass protests, Indonesia rebuilt its presidency after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> in 1998, and many of the states that emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1991 chose presidential systems.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-civil-war.jpg" alt="American Civil War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Gettysburg,_by_Currier_and_Ives.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nathaniel Currier and James Merritt Ives, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The framers argued from accountability and energy. James Wilson held that one executive, “distinguished and responsible”, could be watched and blamed as a council could not, and could act with the speed and secrecy that emergencies demand; Hamilton added that unity, duration in office and adequate powers gave the executive the energy that good government needs. Madison expected the separation of powers to protect liberty by setting each branch’s ambition against the others. Supporters point to the American record: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a>, in force since 1789, is the oldest written national constitution still in use, and its elections have been held on schedule even during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p>Defenders also stress the voter’s direct choice. Electing the head of government directly gives the president a national mandate and the voters a clear choice, and fixed terms bring predictability: there are no sudden cabinet crises, and the executive stays in place through an emergency. Because the legislature does not have to sustain a government, its members can vote against the president’s party without bringing it down; R. Kent Weaver notes that such systems accommodate dissenters and local interests more easily. The separation of powers also gives the legislature reason to scrutinise the executive: investigations by Congress and the courts led to Nixon’s resignation in 1974, and the British politician Woodrow Wyatt remarked that a Watergate could happen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> too, “you just wouldn’t hear about it”.</p><p>Scholars who reject the claim, associated with the political scientist Juan Linz, that presidentialism itself endangers democracy argue that its record depends on its design and setting. Matthew Shugart and John Carey found in 1992 that breakdowns were most common where presidents held strong legislative powers, such as wide powers of decree, and Scott Mainwaring and Shugart argued that presidential systems work well with moderate presidential powers and a small number of reasonably disciplined parties. José Antonio Cheibub argued in 2007 that presidential democracies died more often mainly because they had been adopted in countries with a history of military rule, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>’s presidents since 1985 have governed through coalitions much as prime ministers do, a practice the political scientist Sérgio Abranches called coalition presidentialism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salvador-allende.jpg" alt="Salvador Allende" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salvador_Allende_Gossens-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Biblioteca del Congreso Nacional de Chile, CC BY 3.0 cl</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The most influential critique is Juan Linz’s essay “The Perils of Presidentialism” of 1990. Linz argued that the form builds in a “dual democratic legitimacy”: president and legislature both claim the people’s mandate, and when they clash no democratic principle decides between them, so that deadlock may be broken by force. Fixed terms make government rigid, and presidential elections are zero-sum: “Winners and losers are sharply defined for the entire period of the presidential mandate,” he wrote, and the losers “must wait four or five years without any access to executive power and patronage”. A single office won by a plurality, he added, favours outsiders and tempts presidents to treat opponents as enemies. Linz pointed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, where conflict between president and congress preceded the coups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1964 and in Chile in 1973, when the armed forces overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a>, elected with just over a third of the vote. Fred Riggs observed that presidential systems had slipped into authoritarian rule in almost every country that had tried them, and among the world’s long-established democracies only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Costa Rica were presidential.</p><p>Older critics anticipated these points. Walter Bagehot complained that the American system offered no way to replace a failed leader in a crisis, and that an executive denied the laws it needed, facing a legislature without responsibility, left each blaming the other; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> asked how the nation, like a schoolmaster, was “to know which boy needs the whipping”. Divided government, with a president of one party facing a Congress controlled by another, can end in gridlock. History offers harsher examples. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the first president elected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a>, Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte, barred from a second term, seized power in a coup in December 1851; in 1992 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-fujimori">Alberto Fujimori</a> closed Congress with the army’s support.</p><p>Others criticise the standing of the office. Because the president is both head of state and head of government, the office can attract a reverence that the American scholar Dana Nelson considers harmful to citizens’ own participation in politics. Impeachment, designed for crimes, is a clumsy way to remove a president who has merely lost the country’s support, and cross-national studies, such as John Gerring and Strom Thacker’s of 2004, have found presidential systems associated with more corruption than parliamentary ones.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benito-juarez.jpg" alt="Benito Juárez" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photograph_of_Benito_Juarez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown photographer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Presidential democracy is the characteristic form of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>: almost every country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> elects a president to head its government, as does the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and beyond the Americas the form is found in countries such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and in many African states. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a> inspired constitution-makers well beyond those who copied its presidency, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-rizal">José Rizal</a> in the Philippines and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, although since the 1980s new constitutions have more often looked elsewhere. Many states have chosen a hybrid instead: the semi-presidential model of the French Fifth Republic, with an elected president and a prime minister answerable to parliament, spread widely after 1958, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><p>Within the United States the balance between the branches has shifted repeatedly. The presidency grew with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a>, two world wars and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, until the historian Arthur Schlesinger Jr. warned in 1973 of an “imperial presidency”. Congress responded with the War Powers Resolution of 1973, which requires the president to report to it when sending forces into hostilities, and with new controls over the budget, while the power of judicial review claimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marbury-v-madison">Marbury v. Madison</a> has made the courts a further check on both elected branches.</p><p>Political science has made the comparison one of its longest debates. Linz’s essay launched a large literature on whether presidentialism or parliamentarism better sustains democracy, and later work has moved the question from the form itself to its details: the president’s powers, electoral rules, party systems and each country’s history. Aníbal Pérez-Liñán has shown that since the 1990s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American presidents in crisis have more often been removed by impeachment or forced resignation, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1992 and Venezuela in 1993, than by military coups, which some read as a sign that presidential systems can bend without breaking.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Presidential democracy is government by a president elected separately from the legislature for a fixed term, under a constitution that separates and balances powers. Devised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1787 and in force there since 1789, it became the usual form in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>; its critics, led by Juan Linz, blame it for rigidity and breakdowns, while its defenders credit it with accountability and stability.</em></p>

